<h1>From Essayons to Tetelestai</h1>
<p class="subtitle">Easter Sunday 2026 | John 19:30 | Romans 8:1 | 2 Corinthians 5:21</p>
<p class="phonetic">Tetelestai: teh-TELL-uh-sty</p>

<h2>Opening</h2>
<p>The message is Jesus died and rose 3 days later. Today we celebrate that.</p>
<p>On doing my homework and reflecting, there may be just a bit more. This may not be a traditional Easter message. When Jim asked me to do this, he said: "Follow the heart." I tried to listen to the church and let the Holy Spirit guide.</p>
<p>Hopefully, we will learn a few new words - in French and Aramaic.</p>
<p>I will channel some Pastor Mike and lean on some military background to get to the message. Fortunately for you all, I am ignoring Lindsay and sparing you from some log PT - carrying a large log around.</p>
<p>Point is that He has risen today.</p>
<p>For this I will try to recap Jim and walk us to the "why" I saw this Easter. <span class="pause">[PAUSE]</span></p>

<h2>Opening Prayer</h2>
<p class="prayer">"Heavenly Father, let Your words be mine. Amen."</p>

<h2>Essayons - Let Us Try</h2>
<p>In the Corps of Engineers, our motto is <strong>Essayons</strong> - "Let us try."</p>
<p>It's a humble motto. It doesn't promise success. It promises effort.</p>
<p>Let us try to build the bridge. Let us try to clear the route. Let us try to complete the mission.</p>
<p><strong>Essayons.</strong></p>
<p>For 25 years, that's what I did. We tried. We gave everything we had. And most of the time, we succeeded.</p>
<p>But there's always another mission. Always another obstacle.</p>
<p><strong>Essayons is never finished.</strong> <span class="pause">[PAUSE]</span></p>
<p>However, 2,000 years ago, on a cross outside Jerusalem, a different word was spoken.</p>
<p>Jesus Christ didn't say "I tried."</p>
<p>He said: <strong>"It is finished."</strong></p>
<p>In Greek, one word: <strong>Tetelestai.</strong></p>
<div class="phonetic">
teh (like "tell" without the L)<br>
TEH (same sound, LOUDER - this syllable gets the emphasis)<br>
leh (like "let" without the T)<br>
sty (like "sty" as in pig sty)<br><br>
All together: teh-TELL-uh-sty
</div>
<p>The difference between Essayons and Tetelestai is the difference between human effort and divine accomplishment.</p>
<p>Today, we're going to talk about what Jesus finished - and why it changes everything. <span class="pause">[PAUSE]</span></p>

<h2>The Cost - Recap of Jim's Good Friday Message</h2>
<p>First, we reflect on the cost.</p>
<p>We've been walking through the Passion. Jim really wanted to ensure we knew the cost.</p>
<p>The Last Supper. The Garden of Gethsemane. The arrest. The trials. The beatings.</p>
<p>And last Friday - the crucifixion. <span class="pause">[PAUSE]</span></p>
<p>Let me take you back there. Not to be morbid. But to feel the weight of what happened.</p>
<p>It's Friday morning. Early April in Jerusalem.</p>
<p>The temperature is already climbing. By midday - 85, maybe 90 degrees. Dry heat. No clouds. No breeze. Feels like 103 in March for someone from Arizona.</p>
<p>He carries the wood - roughly 75 to 125 pounds.</p>
<p>They've taken Jesus to a place called Golgotha - "the skull." A rocky hill just outside the city walls. The ground is hard-packed dirt. Stained. This is where Rome executes criminals.</p>
<p>Three crosses. Rough-hewn wood. Not smooth lumber - this is timber cut for function, not comfort. Splinters everywhere.</p>
<p>They lay Jesus on the wood.</p>
<p>His back - already shredded from the flogging Jim described - presses against the rough surface. Every movement drives splinters deeper into torn flesh.</p>
<p>They stretch out His arms. Force iron spikes through His wrists. Not the palms - the bones are too small. Through the wrists, where the bones can hold the weight.</p>
<p>Then His feet. One over the other. Another spike.</p>
<p>The cross is lifted. Dropped into the hole. The jarring impact shattering more of the man. <span class="pause">[PAUSE]</span></p>
<p>In the end it is not the previous torture.</p>
<p>Crucifixion is death by suffocation. The weight of the body pulls down on the arms. The diaphragm can't expand. You can't breathe.</p>
<p>To get air, you have to push up on the nails in your feet. Pull up on the nails in your wrists. Just to fill your lungs.</p>
<p><strong>Every breath is agony.</strong></p>
<p>The sun beats down. No shade. No breeze. Just heat radiating off the rocky ground.</p>
<p>Flies buzzing around the blood.</p>
<p>His lips cracking. His tongue swelling. Dehydration setting in.</p>
<p>Six hours. From nine in the morning until three in the afternoon.</p>
<p>Six hours fighting for every breath. <span class="pause">[PAUSE]</span></p>
<p>And then - at noon - the sky goes dark.</p>
<p>Not a cloud. Not an eclipse. Supernatural darkness.</p>
<p>For three hours, darkness covers the land.</p>
<p>And in that darkness, something happens that had never happened before in all of eternity.</p>
<p><strong>God the Father turns His face away from God the Son.</strong></p>
<p>Because Jesus - who knew no sin - became sin for us. <span class="pause">[LONG PAUSE]</span></p>

<h2>The Divine Plan - Why Only Jesus Could Do This</h2>
<p>Here's what we need to understand.</p>
<p>Divine authority was established in the Garden of Eden. God created Adam and Eve. Gave them one rule. They broke it. Everything fell apart.</p>
<p>We tried to fix it. We couldn't.</p>
<p>Meanwhile, God established the Law. Perfect. Holy. Righteous.</p>
<p>And nobody could keep it.</p>
<h3>Romans 3:23</h3>
<p class="scripture">"All have sinned and fall short of the glory of God."</p>
<p>The Law showed us the problem. But it couldn't fix the problem.</p>
<p>Only one person in all of human history would be able to master the Law. Jesus. Fully God. Fully man. Sinless.</p>
<p>He had to be human - because humans broke it.</p>
<p>He had to be perfect - because a sinner can't pay for sin.</p>
<p>He had to be God - because only an infinite sacrifice can pay an infinite debt.</p>
<p><strong>God designed this plan before the foundation of the world.</strong> <span class="pause">[PAUSE]</span></p>
<p>Something we miss:</p>
<p>Knowing the plan and experiencing the plan are two different things.</p>
<p>God knew this was coming. He designed it. He orchestrated it.</p>
<p><strong>It still hurt.</strong></p>
<p>The betrayal by Judas - one of His own disciples.</p>
<p>The denial by Peter - His friend.</p>
<p>The abandonment - everyone running away.</p>
<p>The mockery - "If you're the Son of God, come down from the cross!"</p>
<p>He felt all of it. And more. <span class="pause">[PAUSE]</span></p>
<p>On that cross, Jesus was burdened with every guilt, every pain, every sorrow, and every sin. A trillion times over.</p>
<p>We feel guilty - we medicate with sin.</p>
<p>We feel pain - we numb it with sin.</p>
<p>We feel sorrow - we escape into sin.</p>
<p><strong>Jesus took ALL of it. A trillion times over.</strong></p>
<p>Your guilt over the thing you did that you can't take back.</p>
<p>Your pain from the wound that won't heal.</p>
<p>Your sorrow from the loss that still aches.</p>
<p>Your sin - the thing you keep doing even though you know it's wrong.</p>
<p><strong>He took it.</strong></p>
<h3>2 Corinthians 5:21</h3>
<p class="scripture">"God made him who had no sin to be sin for us, so that in him we might become the righteousness of God."</p>
<p>Every sin. Every lie ever told. Every act of violence.</p>
<p>Every moment of pride. Every word of gossip.</p>
<p>Every broken promise. Every theft. Every betrayal.</p>
<p>Every murder. Every abuse. Every addiction.</p>
<p>Not only yours. Not just mine.</p>
<p><strong>Everyone's. Past, present, and future.</strong></p>
<p>Every person who ever lived. Every sin ever committed.</p>
<p>A trillion sins. And more. <span class="pause">[LONG PAUSE]</span></p>
<p>And God the Father - holy, perfect, righteous - cannot look at sin.</p>
<p>For all of eternity, the Father and the Son had never been separated. Not for one moment. Perfect unity. Perfect love. Perfect fellowship.</p>
<p>But on that cross - when Jesus became sin - the Father had to turn away.</p>
<p>He couldn't even look at His own Son.</p>
<p>Not because He stopped loving Him.</p>
<p><strong>Because He is holy.</strong></p>
<p>And Jesus - covered in the weight of a trillion sins - was no longer recognizable as the spotless Lamb.</p>
<p>He had become sin itself. And the Father turned His face away.</p>
<p>That's why Jesus cried out: <strong>"My God, my God, why have you forsaken me?"</strong></p>
<p>It wasn't a question. It was the reality. He WAS forsaken. Abandoned. Separated. For the first time in all of eternity.</p>
<p>The loneliness. The isolation. The abandonment. On top of the physical agony. On top of the emotional weight. On top of the spiritual weight of a trillion sins.</p>
<p><strong>That was the cost. That's what Jesus endured to pay your debt.</strong> <span class="pause">[LONG PAUSE]</span></p>
<p>And then - after three hours of darkness - after the wrath was satisfied - after the debt was paid - Jesus spoke one word:</p>
<p><strong>Tetelestai. "It is finished." teh-TELL-uh-sty</strong></p>

<h2>What Does Tetelestai Mean?</h2>
<p>In the ancient world, tetelestai had three meanings:</p>
<p>When a debt was paid, they stamped the receipt: <strong>Tetelestai - PAID IN FULL.</strong></p>
<p>When a soldier completed his mission: <strong>Tetelestai - MISSION ACCOMPLISHED.</strong></p>
<p>When an artist finished a masterpiece: <strong>Tetelestai - IT IS COMPLETE.</strong></p>
<p>Jesus used all three. The debt? Paid in full. The mission? Accomplished. The work? Complete.</p>
<p><strong>Not "I tried." Finished.</strong></p>
<h3>Hebrews 9:12</h3>
<p class="scripture">"He did not enter by means of the blood of goats and calves; but he entered the Most Holy Place once for all by his own blood, thus obtaining eternal redemption."</p>
<p><strong>One sacrifice. Once for all. Forever.</strong> <span class="pause">[PAUSE]</span></p>

<h2>Humanity's Search - Hardwired for Atonement</h2>
<p>For thousands of years, every culture on earth knew something was broken. It's hardwired into our DNA.</p>
<p>The Aztecs knew it. Rivers of blood poured over their altars.</p>
<p>The Greeks knew it. Hundreds of oxen sacrificed at Olympia.</p>
<p>The Egyptians knew it. Bulls and rams offered to silent gods.</p>
<p>Even Israel knew it. Every morning, every evening, the bronze altar burning with sacrifice.</p>
<h3>Romans 1:20</h3>
<p class="scripture">"Since the creation of the world God's invisible qualities - his eternal power and divine nature - have been clearly seen, being understood from what has been made."</p>
<p>Every human knows: We're separated. We owe a debt. Blood must be shed.</p>
<p>Rivers of blood. For thousands of years. But it was never enough.</p>
<h3>Hebrews 10:4</h3>
<p class="scripture">"It is impossible for the blood of bulls and goats to take away sins."</p>
<p>We kept trying. Kept sacrificing.</p>
<p><strong>Essayons. Let us try.</strong></p>
<p>But only Jesus could finish it.</p>
<p>Because only Jesus was human, perfect, and divine.</p>
<p>Only Jesus could bridge the gap. Only Jesus could pay the infinite debt. <span class="pause">[PAUSE]</span></p>

<h2>Easter Sunday - The Victory</h2>
<p>And Sunday morning - everything changed.</p>
<p>The women came to the tomb before dawn. Carrying spices. Expecting to anoint a dead body.</p>
<p>But when they arrived, the stone was rolled away.</p>
<p>Not cracked open. Rolled BACK. The angel sitting on it.</p>
<h3>Matthew 28:5-6</h3>
<p class="scripture">"The angel said to the women, 'Do not be afraid, for I know that you are looking for Jesus, who was crucified. He is not here; he has risen, just as he said.'"</p>
<p><strong>The tomb is empty.</strong></p>
<p>The burial cloths are there - undisturbed - as if the body passed right through them.</p>
<p>And the face cloth that had covered His head - folded neatly. Set aside. Separate from the other cloths. <span class="pause">[PAUSE]</span></p>
<p>Pastor Mike taught us: In Jewish culture, when a master finished a meal, he would crumple his napkin on the table. That signaled to the servants: "I'm done. Clear the table."</p>
<p>But if he folded the napkin and set it aside?</p>
<p><strong>"I'm not finished. I'm coming back."</strong></p>
<p>Jesus folded the face cloth. A clear message:</p>
<p><strong>There will be a second part. I'm coming back.</strong> <span class="pause">[LONG PAUSE]</span></p>
<p>The stone wasn't rolled away so Jesus could get out. He was already gone.</p>
<p><strong>The stone was rolled away so WE could see in.</strong></p>
<p>To see the empty tomb. To see the burial cloths undisturbed. To see the folded napkin. To see the proof.</p>
<p><strong>He is risen. Death is defeated.</strong> <span class="pause">[PAUSE]</span></p>

<h2>The Grace and the Love</h2>
<p>At the end of the day, we all became good enough.</p>
<p>Not because we earned it. Not because we deserved it. Not because we mastered the Law.</p>
<p><strong>Because Jesus finished it.</strong></p>
<p>Even if you mess something up tomorrow - and you will - I will - it's already fixed.</p>
<p>The debt was paid on Friday. The victory was won on Sunday. Today. Forever.</p>
<p>And nothing you do can undo what Jesus did.</p>
<h3>Romans 8:1</h3>
<p class="scripture">"Therefore, there is now no condemnation for those who are in Christ Jesus."</p>
<p>No condemnation. Not "try harder." Not "do better." Not "maybe you'll make it."</p>
<p><strong>You are good enough because HE is good enough.</strong></p>
<p><strong>You are loved because HE loved you first.</strong></p>
<p><strong>You are free because HE finished the work.</strong> <span class="pause">[PAUSE]</span></p>

<h2>From Essayons to Tetelestai</h2>
<p>The Engineer motto is Essayons - "Let us try."</p>
<p>And there's honor in that. Dignity in effort. Nobility in the attempt.</p>
<p>But when it comes to your salvation?</p>
<p><strong>You don't need Essayons. You need Tetelestai.</strong></p>
<p>You don't need to TRY to earn God's love. You already have it.</p>
<p>You don't need to ATTEMPT to pay your debt. It's already paid.</p>
<p>You don't need to STRIVE to be good enough. You already are - because of what Jesus finished.</p>
<p><strong>Essayons says: "Let us try."</strong></p>
<p><strong>Tetelestai says: "It is done."</strong></p>
<p>And on Easter morning, the empty tomb proved it. <span class="pause">[LONG PAUSE]</span></p>

<h2>Closing</h2>
<p>The horror of Friday was real. The pain was real. The cost was real.</p>
<p><strong>But the victory of Sunday is greater.</strong></p>
<p>The tomb is empty. Death is defeated. And you are free.</p>
<p>Not because you tried hard enough.</p>
<p><strong>Because He loves you that much.</strong> <span class="pause">[PAUSE]</span></p>
<p>He looked at the cross. He looked at you. He looked at me.</p>
<p>And He said: <strong>"You're worth this."</strong></p>
<p>The tomb is empty because love is stronger than death.</p>
<p>And if you struggle with the resurrection - if that's hard to believe right now - maybe start here:</p>
<p>A well-documented man laid down his life in a declaration of love for you. He chose you over himself.</p>
<p>It's a little Shakespeare. A little dramatic.</p>
<p>But that man believed in the best in you.</p>
<p>You are free because He chose you.</p>
<p><strong>He is risen.</strong></p>
<p><strong>He is risen indeed.</strong> <span class="pause">[LONG PAUSE]</span></p>
<p>Let's pray.</p>

<h2>Closing Prayer</h2>
<p class="prayer">"Father, thank You for Easter. Thank You that the debt is paid. Thank You that the tomb is empty. Thank You that we don't have to try hard enough - because You already finished it. Help us live in that freedom today. In Jesus' name, Amen."</p>

